{
  "abstract": "We study the independence-three branch of a possible order-twenty-one obstruction to the Earth–Moon problem. Let $G$ be a $K_9$-free $10$-critical graph with 21 vertices, at most 114 edges, and independence number three. We prove that deleting any independent triple cannot leave a 9-critical subgraph of order 13, 14, or 15. Any 16-vertex 9-critical subgraph in the remainder has at most 73 edges. The proof does not use a critical-core census. Exact degree counting reduces each branch to a clique joined to an independent triple, with a bounded number of deleted edges and degree excesses. Graph-polynomial coefficients color most local list assignments; a critical-core rainbow improvement excludes others. Two exceptional list patterns require a different argument: exact bad-list classification forces equality and containment of color lists, and colorings with a ninth color unique to an arbitrary core vertex turn those relations into literal neighborhood relations and a forbidden $K_9$. Independent labeled coverage checks inspect all four finite domains. Polynomial coefficients are checked with separate signed-endpoint algorithms, and the exceptional nine-palette list lemmas have fresh, independently reconstructed and DRAT-checked refutations with positive and negative controls. The portable supplement contains all inputs and verifiers. The result narrows the smaller-core search but does not prove the independent-four-outsider reduction, classify all order-twenty-one critical graphs, or resolve the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem.",
